          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-9b3bdf36989af8ba\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Barfoot directly avows that his love for Rhoda has increased because of the independence and dedication she has just expressed. The core utterance presents his loving state as true. Although it occurs within active courtship, it does not itself clearly undertake a commitment or relational obligation, and there is no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance contributes strategically to Barfoot's courtship, but that interpersonal function does not independently establish performative undertaking force in the core words.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Everard Barfoot directly addresses Rhoda Nunn during their private conversation: “I love you only the more.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal responds to Rhoda's declaration that she will not marry and will remain an example to unmarried working women. Barfoot represents precisely this resolve as increasing his love and admiration. Her concealed look of triumph suggests that the avowal affects or gratifies her, despite her verbal rejection. The wider exchange also frames his declaration as part of persistent and troubling courtship: he has used physical force, speaks of overcoming her resistance, and later assumes that she will yield. Those coercive and strategic elements complicate his conduct but do not alter the core utterance's dominant force as an avowal of an emotional state.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“And for that very reason, because you are capable of such things, I love you only the more.”\",\"supports\":\"The causal wording presents Barfoot's admiration of Rhoda's capabilities as intensifying an existing loving state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rhoda has just defended her resolution never to marry and her work of encouraging women who must live alone; Barfoot replies, “Magnificent!” before making the highlighted avowal.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate exchange identifies the qualities that prompt the avowal and supports its interpretation as a reasoned declaration rather than a reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward, “There was triumph in her look, though she endeavoured to disguise it,” but she tells him to avoid her.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions within courtship and has interpersonal impact, while the response supplies no evidence that its core force is a mutual undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later in the same conversation Barfoot says, “I love you, and I can’t abandon hope.”\",\"supports\":\"His repeated avowal is integrated into a sustained attempt to persuade Rhoda; this supports deliberate truth-conditional presentation rather than an involuntary verbal reflex.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After leaving, Barfoot is described as confident that Rhoda “would yield herself” and determined that her life “should be linked with his.”\",\"supports\":\"This frames the avowal within possessive persistence and attempted courtship, while locating commitment-like or coercive intentions in the surrounding context rather than in the highlighted core utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core force: it is strongly truth-conditional, with its courtship strategy, emotional intensity, and coercive framing captured contextually rather than as additional core categories.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
